  "question": {
    "text": "An organic compound is subjected to chlorination to get compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of chlorine. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is reacted with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AgNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> [Carius Method], the percentage of chlorine in compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is when it forms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3849</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgCl</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;(Round off to the Nearest Integer)<br />(Atomic masses of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>107</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> respectively)"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "19",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi>Cl</mi></msub></math> in compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi>AgCl</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3849</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>107</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>87</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math> mass of chlorine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi>Cl</mi></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0953</mn><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>%</mo></math> wt. of chlorine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0953</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>06</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p>"
